.\" satl-term(1) - manual page for the GUI terminal.
.\"
.\" Pure ASCII, for the reason given at the top of satl.1.
.\"
.\" The point this page has to land is that satl-term is not "satl with a
.\" window": it is a different binary with a different dependency set, and the
.\" whole reason it exists is that the interpreter must not carry them.
.TH SATL\-TERM 1 2026-08-07 "satellite 0.1" "Satellite Manual"
.SH NAME
satl\-term \- graphical terminal window for the satellite interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B satl\-term
.RI [ file " [" args ...]]
.br
.B satl\-term
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B satl\-term
opens a GTK4 window containing one VTE terminal and spawns the interpreter,
.BR satl (1),
into that terminal's pseudo\-terminal. With no arguments the child is started as
.BR "satl \-\-repl" ;
given a
.IR file ,
the child is
.B satl \-\-run
.I file
.IR args ...,
and the window shows the program's output.
.PP
It interprets nothing itself. It renders the bytes its child writes and passes
keystrokes back, and that is the whole of it.
.SS Why this is a separate program
The obvious design is one binary that opens a window when it has no file to run.
It was measured and rejected. Linking GTK4 and VTE into the interpreter made the
dynamic linker load
.B 119
shared objects \(em pango, harfbuzz, cairo, gdk\-pixbuf and a hundred more \(em
before
.B main()
ran, on every invocation, including the headless
.B satl \-\-run
that touches none of them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
satl \-\-run hello.satl, linked against gtk4          25.9 ms
the same interpreter, not linked against gtk4        2.5 ms
a bare int main(){return 0;}                         2.2 ms
.EE
.RE
.PP
So 23.4 ms of a 25.9 ms run was the loader, for libraries the run never called
into, against an interpreter whose own share of hello world is 0.3 ms. Split in
two, the window keeps its 119 shared objects and the interpreter keeps its 6,
and neither pays for the other; the same benchmark went from 1.3\(mu slower than
.BR python3 (1)
to 7.6\(mu faster, with peak resident memory falling from 26.3 MB to 4.0 MB.
.PP
The split is nearly free because the two\-process architecture predates it. The
window never interpreted anything anyway \(em it always spawned a child into the
terminal's pseudo\-terminal \(em so the only thing that changed is which binary
it spawns: the sibling
.B satl
rather than itself.
.PP
The pseudo\-terminal is also what makes the window a real terminal rather than a
text widget. Echo, backspace, line editing, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D and SIGWINCH are
the kernel's tty line discipline, obtained free by having a child on the other
end of a pty; a single\-process design would have to reimplement every one of
them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.IR file " [" args ...]
Run
.I file
in the window instead of opening the REPL.
.I file
and
.I args
are handed to the child unchanged, so they mean exactly what they mean in
.BR "satl \-\-run" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary on standard error and exit 2.
.SH NOTES
.B satl\-term
locates the interpreter by reading
.I /proc/self/exe
and looking for a file named
.B satl
in the same directory. The two binaries are therefore installed side by side in
.IR $prefix/bin ,
and copying
.B satl\-term
somewhere on its own will not work. This is deliberate: resolving the sibling by
path rather than by
.B PATH
means the window always runs the interpreter it was installed with, not whatever
older
.B satl
happens to come first in the environment it was launched from.
.PP
This program owns the only window involved. The interpreter it spawns links no
GUI libraries at all, and the language has no window functions yet, so a
satellite program cannot open a window of its own \(em in this terminal or
anywhere else. The design that would give it one loads GTK from a shared object
at the moment a program asks for it, so that
.B satl
keeps, for every program that never asks, the startup it gained by not linking
GTK.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
.TQ
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
The usual GTK display variables. Without one of them there is no window to open
and
.B satl\-term
has nothing to do; use
.B satl \-\-run
directly, which needs no display server at all.
.PP
.B SATELLITE_PATH
is read by the interpreter, not by this program, and is inherited by the child
along with the rest of the environment. See
.BR satl (1).
.SH FILES
Paths are shown for the default prefix,
.IR /usr/local .
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/satl\-term
This program.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/satl
The interpreter it spawns, found as this binary's sibling.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/applications/org.satellite.terminal.desktop
The desktop entry, so the window can be started from a menu or launcher. It is
named after the application id this program registers, because that is the name
a desktop shell looks the entry up by when it wants to know which launcher an
open window belongs to.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
.EX
$ satl\-term
$ satl\-term hello.satl
$ satl\-term bench.satl 100000
.EE
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The window was closed normally.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.
.PP
Otherwise the status is whatever the GTK application loop returned. The
interpreter's exit status is
.I not
propagated \(em it is a child inside the terminal, and its status is reported in
the terminal where the user can read it. To get a program's exit status, run it
under
.BR satl (1)
instead.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR satl (1)
.SH AUTHOR
The satellite project <terransatellite@gmail.com>.
